# Summary
Introduced: presigned session credentials are now accepted, but the presigned path still returns streaming: None and lacks the header path's reserved-session STREAMING-* rejection. This can bypass aws-chunked per-chunk integrity checks for session presigned streaming uploads. A robust fix would either reject reserved session credentials with STREAMING-* payload hashes in authenticate_presigned until streaming session support is implemented, or build the appropriate StreamingSigningContext for presigned requests and require the decoder to verify signed chunk modes.
This commit enables presigned authentication for reserved session access keys, but unlike the existing header-auth session path it does not reject STREAMING-AWS4-HMAC-SHA256 payload modes or construct an AuthContext.streaming value. The streaming PUT path classifies x-amz-content-sha256=STREAMING-AWS4-HMAC-SHA256-* as a signed aws-chunked upload, but it passes auth.streaming into the chunk decoder. For presigned requests that value is always None, and the decoder only requires and verifies per-chunk signatures when its streaming context is Some. As a result, a presigned session request for a signed streaming upload can reach the decoder with no chunk-signature enforcement, allowing body chunks to be omitted, altered, or supplied without valid chunk signatures after the seed presigned request authenticates. The header session path has an explicit guard for this unsupported mode; the presigned session path added by this commit lacks the equivalent guard.

# Resolution

Fixed in `32564884`.

The integrity issue was valid, but the proposed alternatives and part of the
attribution were not AWS-compatible. The parent of `0e62ab6` already allowed
long-lived presigned credentials to reach the same missing-context decoder
path; `0e62ab6` expanded its reach to temporary credentials. The reported
header-session rejection was also removed by later Phase 2 work after proper
header aws-chunked session support was implemented.

Live AWS probes were added before choosing the fix. They establish that
presigned SigV4 does not activate aws-chunked decoding for long-lived or
temporary credentials, even when `x-amz-content-sha256` is a supported
`STREAMING-*` marker and every streaming-related header is signed. AWS treats
the request body as raw bytes:

- signed wire bodies return the exact `IncompleteBody` response based on the
  declared decoded length and raw wire length, regardless of whether chunk
  signatures are valid, invalid, or absent;
- exact-length raw PutObject bodies return `XAmzContentSHA256Mismatch`, treating
  the marker as the literal claimed hash;
- trailer-marker PutObject bodies return `IncompleteBody` when short, the hash
  mismatch when exact, and generic `InternalError` when overlong or framed;
- UploadPart was probed separately against live multipart uploads. Trailer
  bodies are `IncompleteBody` when short and `MalformedTrailerError` when
  exact, overlong, or framed. ListParts after every failure confirmed that no
  part was stored.
- signed HeadObject checks after every failed PutObject confirmed that no
  object was stored, including after each generic AWS `InternalError`.

The production fix carries the authenticated mode into PutObject and
UploadPart body handling. Header SigV4 continues through the production
incremental aws-chunked decoder. Presigned SigV4 instead takes the AWS-pinned
raw count/hash path and never decodes or accepts the framed bytes. PutObject
does not start a write session and UploadPart aborts its prepared session, so
none of these errors can commit an object or part.

Argmin intentionally maps the client-triggerable PutObject trailer overrun to
`400 MalformedTrailerError`, rather than AWS's `500 InternalError`. This follows
the repository-wide rule that malformed client input must not activate the
panic/abort-on-500 operational policy; the explicit exception is recorded in
the AWS compatibility guide. UploadPart already uses that 400 response on AWS.

As a defense-in-depth invariant, constructing either signed decoder mode now
requires `StreamingSigningContext`; absence is an internal
authentication/adapter invariant failure, preserving fail-on-500 diagnostics
instead of hiding a regression as a client signature error. The duplicate
test-only batch decoder was removed. Decoder tests now feed and finish the same
incremental decoder used by production.

Deterministic verification:

- `env STS_TEST_PRESIGNED_STREAMING_ONLY=1 ./scripts/aws-sts-oracle --assume-role`
- `cargo nextest run -p server-http presigned_streaming`
- `cargo nextest run -p server-http signed_streaming_modes_without_context_are_internal_invariant_failures`

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the existing header-auth session path rejects reserved session credentials with STREAMING-* payload hashes.
- [x] Confirm the presigned path accepts/resolves reserved session access keys and lacks an equivalent STREAMING-* rejection.
- [x] Dynamically demonstrate a valid presigned session request with `x-amz-content-sha256=STREAMING-AWS4-HMAC-SHA256-PAYLOAD` authenticates and returns `streaming: None`.
- [x] Dynamically demonstrate the aws-chunked decoder accepts chunks without `chunk-signature` when `StreamingSigningContext` is absent, while rejecting the same body when it is present.
- [x] Tie the authenticated context to the server streaming path that passes `ctx.streaming_signing.as_ref()` into the decoder for signed chunked modes.
## Report
Validated the finding as a logic/integrity vulnerability. Crash reproduction was not applicable because this is not a memory-safety bug; targeted Rust tests completed without crashing. Valgrind/gdb were unavailable in the container (`valgrind not installed`, `gdb not installed`), but lldb was available and used non-interactively.

Evidence:
1. Header-auth session path rejects reserved session access keys with STREAMING-* payload hashes at `crates/auth/src/request.rs:368-373`.
2. Presigned path resolves reserved session credentials at `crates/auth/src/request.rs:680-687` and `785-792`, uses the `x-amz-content-sha256` header as the presigned payload hash at `701-704`, but returns `AuthContext { streaming: None }` at `751-762`.
3. Added targeted auth PoC tests. Command: `cargo test -p auth poc_ -- --nocapture`. Output showed the asymmetry:
   - `POC header session STREAMING result: UnknownAccessKey { access_key_id: "ARGS0123456789ABCDEFGHIJ" }`
   - `POC presigned session STREAMING authenticated: mode=PresignedSigV4 access_key=Some("ARGS0123456789ABCDEFGHIJ") streaming_present=false`
   Both tests passed.
4. Added targeted decoder PoC test. Command: `cargo test -p server-http poc_signed_upload_with_missing_streaming_context_accepts_unsigned_chunks -- --nocapture`. Output showed unsigned aws-chunked data is accepted when the streaming signing context is absent, and rejected when present:
   - `POC decoder without StreamingSigningContext accepted 5 bytes: "PWNED"; done=true`
   - `POC decoder with StreamingSigningContext rejected same wire: MalformedChunkedBody { reason: "missing chunk-signature in signed chunked upload" }`
5. lldb trace: breakpoint at `request.rs:751` in `authenticate_presigned` was hit for the presigned session STREAMING PoC. Backtrace showed `authenticate_presigned -> authenticate_request -> poc_presigned_session_streaming_payload_hash_is_accepted_without_streaming_context`; source context at the breakpoint was the `Ok(AuthContext { ... })` construction, and after continuing the test printed `streaming_present=false`.

The server streaming path classifies `STREAMING-AWS4-HMAC-SHA256-PAYLOAD` as signed aws-chunked at `crates/server-http/src/http/serve.rs:1996-2024` and creates the decoder with `ctx.streaming_signing.as_ref()` at `serve.rs:3104-3107`. The decoder only requires chunk-signature fields if `self.streaming.is_some()` at `crates/server-http/src/http/chunked.rs:500-505`, and only verifies HMACs inside `if let Some(ref ctx) = self.streaming` at `591-607`. Therefore the presigned session path can authenticate a signed streaming upload while supplying no streaming signing context to downstream chunk verification.

